// introduction
Wood, though one of the oldest structural materials, is still one of the most common and useful ones. It is heavily utilized by plants, being necessary for the height to which trees are able to grow to. Furthermore, it is central to modern engineering and construction.

Wood possesses some key advantages for these purposes: its high strength-to-weight ratio, low density, ease of machining, abundance, and renewability. Wood is also an excellent thermal and acoustic insulator. These qualities lead to wood being most widely used in buildings, furniture, and other structures.

That is not to say wood has no disadvantages. Wood is anisotropic, or has strength varying on direction of load. This results from its composition: compacted cellulose fibers bound together by hemicellulose and lignin. The cellulose fibers are very strong and thus wood is much stronger along the grain. Perpendicular to the grain, wood appears weak and brittle in comparison. This limits how wood can be oriented, connected, and loaded. It is therefore prone to failing at points where loads press across the grain.

As purely mechanical and chemical methods to strengthening wood by densification sacrifice many important qualities to work, hybrid methods are necessary. Thermo-mechanical compression is safer and cheaper, but suffers from the springback effect, which is only partially mitigated by the heating process. Since lignin causes springback, a delignifying pre-treatment could substantially lower springback and internal stress while also improving maximum compression.

// engineering goal
The final goal of this project is to produce high-density, high-strength wood using a thermo-mechanical densification process derived from that of Song et al. (2018). This study specifically aims to optimize this process for minimization of cost and resource usage while retaining the significant mechanical strength and toughness gains seen in previous literature. The data will also explore the weight reduction capabilities of lignin removal and its effect on the generation of a weight-effective structural material.
